.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt PROVIDER-OBJECT 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm provider-object
.Nd A specification for a provider-native object abstraction
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/core_object.h
.In openssl/core_names.h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The provider-native object abstraction is a set of
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
keys and values that can be used to pass provider-native objects to
OpenSSL library code or between different provider operation
implementations with the help of OpenSSL library code.
.Pp
The intention is that certain provider-native operations can pass any
sort of object that belong with other operations, or with OpenSSL
library code.
.Pp
An object may be passed in the following manners:
.Bl -enum
.It
.Em \&By value
.Pp
This means that the
.Em object data
is passed as an octet string or an UTF8 string, which can be handled in
diverse ways by other provided implementations.
The encoding of the object depends on the context it's used in; for
example,
.Xr OSSL_DECODER 3
allows multiple encodings, depending on existing decoders.
If central OpenSSL library functionality is to handle the data directly,
it
.Sy must
be encoded in DER for all object types except for
.Sy OSSL_OBJECT_NAME
(see
.Sx Parameter reference
below), where it's assumed to a plain UTF8 string.
.It
.Em \&By reference
.Pp
This means that the
.Em object data
isn't passed directly, an
.Em object reference
is passed instead.
It's an octet string that only the correct provider understands
correctly.
.El
.Pp
Objects
.Em by value
can be used by anything that handles DER encoded objects.
.Pp
Objects
.Em by reference
need a higher level of cooperation from the implementation where the
object originated (let's call it X) and its target implementation (let's
call it Y):
.Bl -enum
.It
.Em \&An object loading function in the target implementation
.Pp
The target implementation (Y) may have a function that can take an
.Em object reference .
This can only be used if the target implementation is from the same
provider as the one originating the object abstraction in question (X).
.Pp
The exact target implementation to use is determined from the
.Em object type
and possibly the
.Em object data type .
For example, when the OpenSSL library receives an object abstraction
with the
.Em object type
.Sy OSSL_OBJECT_PKEY ,
it will fetch a
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7
using the
.Em object data type
as its key type (the second argument in
.Xr EVP_KEYMGMT_fetch 3 Ns ).
.It
.Em \&An object exporter in the originating implementation
.Pp
The originating implementation (X) may have an exporter function.
This exporter function can be used to export the object in
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
form, that can then be imported by the target implementation's imported
function.
.Pp
This can be used when it's not possible to fetch the target
implementation (Y) from the same provider.
.El
.Ss Parameter reference
A provider-native object abstraction is an
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
with a selection of the following parameters:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqdata" ( Ns Sy OSSL_OBJECT_PARAM_DATA Ns ) <octet string> or <UTF8 string>
The object data
.Em passed by value .
.It \(dqreference" ( Ns Sy OSSL_OBJECT_PARAM_REFERENCE Ns ) <octet string>
The object data
.Em passed by reference .
.It \(dqtype" ( Ns Sy OSSL_OBJECT_PARAM_TYPE Ns ) <integer>
The
.Em object type ,
a number that may have any of the following values (all defined in
.Pa <openssl/core_object.h>
):
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy OSSL_OBJECT_NAME
The object data may only be
.Em passed by value ,
and should be a UTF8 string.
.Pp
This is useful for
.Xr provider-storemgmt 7
when a URI load results in new URIs.
.It Sy OSSL_OBJECT_PKEY
The object data is suitable as provider-native
.Sy EVP_PKEY
key data.
The object data may be
.Em passed by value
or
.Em passed by reference .
.It Sy OSSL_OBJECT_CERT
The object data is suitable as
.Sy X509
data.
The object data for this object type can only be
.Em passed by value ,
and should be an octet string.
.Pp
Since there's no provider-native X.509 object, OpenSSL libraries that
receive this object abstraction are expected to convert the data to a
.Sy X509
object with
.Xr d2i_X509 3 .
.It Sy OSSL_OBJECT_CRL
The object data is suitable as
.Sy X509_CRL
data.
The object data can only be
.Em passed by value ,
and should be an octet string.
.Pp
Since there's no provider-native X.509 CRL object, OpenSSL libraries
that receive this object abstraction are expected to convert the data to
a
.Sy X509_CRL
object with
.Xr d2i_X509_CRL 3 .
.El
.It \(dqdata-type" ( Ns Sy OSSL_OBJECT_PARAM_DATA_TYPE Ns ) <UTF8 string>
The specific type of the object content.
Legitimate values depend on the object type; if it is
.Sy OSSL_OBJECT_PKEY ,
the data type is expected to be a key type suitable for fetching a
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7
that can handle the data.
.It \(dqdata-structure" ( Ns Sy OSSL_OBJECT_PARAM_DATA_STRUCTURE Ns ) <UTF8 string>
The outermost structure of the object content.
Legitimate values depend on the object type.
.It \(dqdesc" ( Ns Sy OSSL_OBJECT_PARAM_DESC Ns ) <UTF8 string>
A human readable text that describes extra details on the object.
.El
.Pp
When a provider-native object abstraction is used, it
.Em must
contain object data in at least one form (object data
.Em passed by value ,
i.e.
the "data" item, or object data
.Em passed by reference ,
i.e.
the "reference" item).
Both may be present at once, in which case the OpenSSL library code that
receives this will use the most optimal variant.
.Pp
For objects with the object type
.Sy OSSL_OBJECT_NAME ,
that object type
.Em must
be given.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr provider 7 ,
.Xr OSSL_DECODER 3
.Sh HISTORY
The concept of providers and everything surrounding them was introduced
in OpenSSL 3.0.
